GPL does not prevent sale of software, as Titus pointed out you do need
to provide the code if you do. There are also plenty of GPL compatible
licenses you could write a package under.

Data is not covered under the GPL, so yes you can continue to charge for
access to the data the packages uses.

Your employer should pay a lawyer to work out the issues around the
licensing.

There are plenty of options that are viable.

Enjoy,
Alex Mandel, PhD

Center for Spatial Sciences
   http://spatial.ucdavis.edu
Geospatial and Farming Systems Research Consortium
   http://gfc.ucdavis.edu
University of California, Davis

On 11/01/2017 08:37 AM, C. Titus Brown wrote:
> Hi Jacob & Noam,
>
> here's a not-irrelevant stackexchange Q&A:
>
> https://opensource.stackexchange.com/questions/4414/if-my-r-package-uses-gpl-packages-does-mine-automatically-inherit-gpl
>
> I'm not 100% sure I understand the details, but here's my 2c:
>
> * if you distribute GPL licensed packages in any way shape or form, you MUST
>    include access to the source code of those packages;
>
> * if you distribute software that includes GPL licensed code (i.e. your R
>    package?) you must also make the source code available for all the software
>    that depends on that GPL licensed code.  This is the contaminatory aspect of
>    GPL.
>
> I'm not a lawyer, tho, and that's pretty much who you need to talk to about
> this.
>
> cheers,
> --titus
>
> On Wed, Nov 01, 2017 at 03:30:15PM +0000, Noam Ross wrote:
>> Jacob,
>>
>> Private companies write and sell private R packages all the time.  My
>> understanding is that the key distinction is that your R package *links to*
>> R and other packages, but *does not include code from them.* You and your
>> company are not reusing, modifying, or distributing the code from your
>> dependencies (and R itself) that are GPL-licensed,  so it's fine that your
>> private, commercial package depends on them.  It would be a different story
>> if you, say, copied code from those other packages to include in yours.  Then
>> you would be obligated to follow the terms of GPL which require you to
>> release your code under a compatible share-alike license.
>>
>> I'm always wary when I wade into these discussions on licenses, as they can
>> be complex and I'm no expert.  But I'm sure someone will point out where
>> I'm wrong :)
>>
>> Noam
>>
